@page
@model AboutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "About";
}

<div class="page-head">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="page-sub">What this is, and where the numbers come from.</p>
</div>

<p class="lede">
    Utah Course Compass puts the University of Utah&rsquo;s class schedule and its
    published grade history in one place, so you can see how a course has actually
    graded &mdash; and how the professor teaching it grades that same course &mdash;
    before you register.
</p>

<div class="stat-row four">
    <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-value">@Model.Courses.ToString("N0")</span>
        <span class="stat-label">Courses</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-value">@Model.Sections.ToString("N0")</span>
        <span class="stat-label">Sections</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-value">@Model.Instructors.ToString("N0")</span>
        <span class="stat-label">Instructors</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
        <span class="stat-value">@Model.GradedSections.ToString("N0")</span>
        <span class="stat-label">Graded sections</span>
    </div>
</div>

<h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
<p>
    Schedules come from the registrar&rsquo;s public class schedule, through
    @(Model.LastScheduleTerm is { } t ? Terms.Display(t) : "the current term").
    Grades come from the University&rsquo;s public grade summary, covering
    @(Model.FirstGradeTerm is { } a ? Terms.Display(a) : "") through
    @(Model.LastGradeTerm is { } b ? Terms.Display(b) : "").
    Both are published by the University; nothing here is private, and no grade
    belonging to an individual student appears anywhere in it.
</p>

<h2>How to read an average</h2>
<p>
    A course average is the mean grade point across everyone who took it, so a
    2.6 means the typical student earned a B&minus;/C+. Two things are worth
    knowing before you lean on one.
</p>
<ul>
    <li>
        <strong>Small groups are hidden.</strong> The University withholds any
        grade group with fewer than five students, but those students still count
        toward the published average. So a distribution can look better than the
        average beside it. Where the gap is large, the course page says so.
    </li>
    <li>
        <strong>An average is not a difficulty score.</strong> Who enrolls matters
        as much as who teaches. A required first-year course and a senior elective
        are not measured on the same scale.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2>Unofficial</h2>
<p>
    This is a student project, not a University service. It is not affiliated with,
    endorsed by, or maintained by the University of Utah. Seat counts and times can
    lag the registrar, and only the official
    <a href="https://student.utah.edu/" rel="noopener">student portal</a> can
    register you for anything. Check there before you rely on what you see here.
</p>
